✅ Q1: Identify Interface Segregation Violation + Solution
Problem

The Bird interface forces all birds to implement fly(), but some birds (Penguins) cannot fly.

🔍 Violated Principle

👉 Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
“No class should be forced to depend on methods it does not use.”

Penguin is forced to implement fly() → throws exception → violation.

✅ Correct Refactored Solution
Step 1 — Split interfaces
interface EggLayer {
    void layEggs();
}

interface Flyable {
    void fly();
}

Step 2 — Bird base class (optional)
class Bird { }

Step 3 — Sparrow: can fly + lays eggs
class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable, EggLayer {

    @Override
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Sparrow flying...");
    }

    @Override
    public void layEggs() {
        System.out.println("Sparrow laying eggs...");
    }
}

Step 4 — Penguin: lays eggs but does NOT fly
class Penguin extends Bird implements EggLayer {

    @Override
    public void layEggs() {
        System.out.println("Penguin laying eggs...");
    }
}


✔ No forced methods
✔ No exceptions
✔ ISP fully satisfied

✅ Q2: Shopping Cart – Product Class + Total Calculation
Complete Program
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Scanner;

class Product {
    String name;
    double price;
    int quantity;

    public Product(String name, double price, int quantity) {
        this.name = name;
        this.price = price;
        this.quantity = quantity;
    }

    public double getTotal() {
        return price * quantity;
    }
}

class ShoppingCart {
    private List<Product> cart = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addProduct(Product p) {
        cart.add(p);
    }

    public double calculateTotal() {
        double total = 0;
        for (Product p : cart) {
            total += p.getTotal();
        }
        return total;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        ShoppingCart cart = new ShoppingCart();

        System.out.print("How many products to add? ");
        int n = sc.nextInt();

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            System.out.print("Name: ");
            String name = sc.next();
            System.out.print("Price: ");
            double price = sc.nextDouble();
            System.out.print("Quantity: ");
            int qty = sc.nextInt();

            cart.addProduct(new Product(name, price, qty));
        }

        System.out.println("Total Amount = " + cart.calculateTotal());
    }
}


✔ Simple
✔ Works in terminal
✔ Perfect for lab exam

✅ Q3: Identify Code Smells + Refactor
Given Problem
public class OrderManager {
    private List<Order> orders;

    public void processOrders() {
        for (Order order : orders) {
            // Validate order
            if (order.getAmount() > 0) {
                System.out.println("Processing order for " + order.getCustomerName());

                // Send confirmation email
                System.out.println("Sending confirmation email to " + order.getCustomerEmail());

            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid order amount!");
            }
        }
    }
}

🔍 Code Smells Identified
1. Long Method / Too many responsibilities

processOrders() does validation + processing + sending emails → violates SRP.

2. Feature Envy

OrderManager is printing email logic → should be in separate class.

3. No separation of concerns

Business logic mixed with I/O (println).

🔧 Refactored Solution (Clean, SRP, Maintainable)
Step 1 — Create Validator
class OrderValidator {
    public boolean isValid(Order order) {
        return order.getAmount() > 0;
    }
}

Step 2 — Create Email Service
class EmailService {
    public void sendConfirmation(String email) {
        System.out.println("Sending confirmation email to " + email);
    }
}

Step 3 — Clean OrderManager
class OrderManager {

    private List<Order> orders;
    private OrderValidator validator = new OrderValidator();
    private EmailService emailService = new EmailService();

    public void processOrders() {
        for (Order order : orders) {

            if (validator.isValid(order)) {

                System.out.println("Processing order for " + order.getCustomerName());
                emailService.sendConfirmation(order.getCustomerEmail());

            } else {
                System.out.println("Invalid order amount!");
            }
        }
    }
}
